docs {
  # GET /v1/admin/health

  Operator server-health readout — the JSON counterpart of `GET /metrics` for
  the companion app's per-relay Status screen. Literal facts only; the endpoint
  derives no ok/warn verdicts. Admin-authed: the master token (shown here)
  **or** an active operator companion-app device's signed request.

  Response:
  - `version`, `uptimeSeconds` — process facts.
  - `accounts` — `{total, active, deactivated, suspended, takendown, flagged}`;
    derived lifecycle buckets (takendown > suspended > deactivated precedence,
    matching the account-listing `status` filter) that partition `total`
    exactly, plus `flagged` — accounts carrying at least one in-force label
    from a watched labeler (orthogonal to the lifecycle buckets; always 0 when
    labeler watching is off). `flagged` is the console's badge count.
  - `storage` — `{blobCount, blobBytes, blobUnownedCount, blobUnownedBytes,
    blockCount}`; physical rows (blobs are shared across owners and counted
    once). `blobUnownedCount`/`blobUnownedBytes` are the subset of those rows
    that **no** `blob_owners` row claims — stored blob rows nobody owns. Every other
    operator-reachable blob surface (the account listing, per-account storage,
    `listBlobs`, `listMissingBlobs`, `getBlob`) resolves through `blob_owners`,
    so all of them report the same zero whether an account's ownership rows were
    lost or its blobs were reclaimed; this pair is the only place the difference
    is visible server-wide. It counts rows: whether those rows' files are present
    and hash correctly is `sweeps.blobScrub`'s question, not this pair's.

    Expect ~0: GC deletes a physical row with its last owner, so a row is unowned
    only between those two statements. A value that **survives across sweeps** is
    the alarm — and unlike a stale `blobGc.completedAt`, it does not require the
    sweep to have stopped in order to show.
  - `firehose` — `{currentSeq, subscribers, retainedEvents,
    backfillWindowSeconds}`; `backfillWindowSeconds` is the age of the oldest
    retained event (`null` when the log is empty).
  - `sweeps` — `{blobGc, blobMirror, blobScrub, firehoseGc, accountReaper,
    agentClaimSweep, adminNonceSweep, spaceJtiSweep, spaceOplogSweep,
    labelerWatch}`, each
    `{completedAt, swept, errors}` (unix seconds, items acted on, failures hit)
    or `null` until that sweep's first completed pass after boot. `blobMirror`
    stays `null` while the mirror is not configured and `labelerWatch` while
    labeler watching is off, both like an idle sweep; `blobMirror`'s `swept`
    counts bucket objects synced (uploads + deletes), `blobScrub`'s counts
    unhealed integrity problems flagged that pass, and `labelerWatch`'s counts
    label rows changed.

    A sweep reports two **independent** faults, because they call for different
    responses. A pass that fails outright records nothing, so a stale
    `completedAt` means passes are not completing at all — the sweep is dead. A
    fresh `completedAt` carrying a nonzero `errors` means the pass ran and
    skipped part of its work: for blob GC, an account whose reconcile failed is
    excluded from the sweep and leaks disk until the fault is fixed. `errors`
    counts failed *operations*, not the work they cost — one failed account is
    1, however many of its blobs go uncollected — so it is a count of broken
    subjects, never a severity. Sweeps with no per-item error path always
    report 0.

    Both are literal facts, per this endpoint's no-verdicts rule; the thresholds
    that turn either into an alarm belong to the client.
}
